WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.869
Idea. A shop bill is price times quantity. If the bill is zero, the price is zero or the quantity is zero. In maths too: if two things multiply to give zero, one of them is zero.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:25.375
Factorising means writing an expression as a product. Look for a common factor, a part in every term. In two sine y cosine y plus sine y, sine y is common.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:37.936
Tan is sine over cosine, so it is undefined when cosine is zero. That means at ninety degrees and two hundred and seventy degrees. Those angles can never be solutions.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:47.501
This question type gives a trig equation with a common factor. You factorise, solve each factor, and keep every solution that is allowed.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:01:02.228
Method. Step one: write tan as sine over cosine, or use the given identity, so every term has a common factor. Step two: factorise. Do not divide, because dividing loses solutions.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:14.958
Step three: set each factor equal to zero and solve each simple equation in the interval. Step four: combine all the solutions, and remove any angle where tan is undefined.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:23.531
The trap is dividing both sides by the common factor. It looks neat, but it loses the solutions where that factor is zero.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:40.190
Worked example. January twenty twenty. Giving your solutions to one decimal place where appropriate, solve two tan y degrees plus five sine y degrees equals zero, for y from zero to one hundred and eighty. Four marks.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:50.637
Write tan as sine over cosine. Two sine y over cosine y, plus five sine y, equals zero. Every term now has sine y.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:02:06.598
Take out sine y. Sine y, times, two over cosine y plus five, equals zero. Multiply the bracket by cosine y to clear the fraction: sine y, times, two plus five cosine y, equals zero.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:19.369
Two factors, so two equations. Sine y equals zero gives y equals zero, or y equals one hundred and eighty. Both are in the interval, and tan is defined at both.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:36.144
Two plus five cosine y equals zero gives cosine y equals minus two over five. Cosine inverse of minus nought point four is one hundred and thirteen point six degrees. The other solution is above one eighty.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:48.101
A tempting wrong route is to divide both sides by sine y. That leaves one solution only. It throws away y equals zero and y equals one hundred and eighty.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:59.548
So y equals zero, one hundred and thirteen point six, or one hundred and eighty. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:14.205
Common mistake. A common mistake is a sign slip. Five cosine y equals minus two. If the minus is lost, cosine y is plus two fifths, and y comes out as sixty six point four.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:35.650
Your turn. Now you try, from January twenty twenty three. From part B, sine two theta minus tan theta is tan theta cosine two theta. Solve sine two x degrees minus tan x degrees equals zero, for x from nought to three sixty, not included. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:48.028
Answer. Use the part B result: tan x times cosine two x equals zero. Two factors, so tan x equals zero, or cosine two x equals zero.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:04:06.747
Tan x equals zero gives x equals one eighty. Nought and three sixty are not allowed. Cosine two x equals zero gives two x equals ninety, two seventy, four fifty, six thirty. So x is forty five, one thirty five, two twenty five, three fifteen.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:16.481
The most common wrong answer divides by tan x. That leaves only the four cosine solutions, and misses x equals one hundred and eighty.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:24.019
Remember: factorise, never divide. Solve each factor, combine, and remove undefined angles.
